  year        = {1958},
  note        = {Later reissued as NASA TR R-11. 103 pages},
  annote  = {THE ROUND 0 CITATION. Read the Summary p1 before drafting Section 1: it
             already contains the structure Section 4 claims, in 1958
             vocabulary. Chapman reduces the pair of entry equations over an
             exponential atmosphere to a SINGLE second-order nonlinear ODE --
             the Z function -- by dropping two small terms and applying one
             transformation. Then, in his own words: dropping the gravity,
             centrifugal and lift terms leaves a LINEAR equation whose solution
             is PRECISELY that of allen1958study for steep ballistic entry;
             dropping instead the vertical acceleration and the vertical
             component of drag leaves a truncated equation whose solution is
             PRECISELY Saenger's equilibrium glide. One equation, two named
             distinguished limits, recovering the two regimes Paper 1 calls the
             bubble and the skeleton. The claim Paper 1 may make is therefore
             NOT that the decomposition is new. What is new is that Chapman's
             solutions are UNIVERSAL SINGLE TRAJECTORIES -- one solution per
             lift-to-drag ratio, determining the motion of a vehicle of
             arbitrary weight and shape -- with no error bound, no set of
             trajectories, and no statement about initial-condition ensembles.
             The contribution is the passage from an approximate trajectory to a
             certified outer approximation of a reachable set. Say that
             explicitly and cite this Summary for the concession.},
}

